---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import PlaceholderPage from '../../../components/PlaceholderPage.astro'
import { company, contact, mailto } from '../../../data/site'

// Written from what the app does as of build 0.1.0 (November 2026). Update it if the app gains
// analytics, accounts, a server of ours, or sends anything new off the device.
const updated = '4 November 2026'
const h2 = 'pt-6 text-2xl leading-tight font-heavy text-coral-ink xl:text-d31'
const link = 'underline underline-offset-4'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vinyl Corner privacy policy | Bit More Media"
  description="What the Vinyl Corner app does with your information: it collects nothing for us."
>
  <PlaceholderPage title="Vinyl Corner privacy policy">
    <p>Last updated {updated}.</p>
    <p>
      In short: Vinyl Corner doesn’t collect any information about you for us. There’s no account
      with us, no analytics, no advertising and no tracking, and the app doesn’t send anything to a
      server of ours. Your music library is read on your device, and anything the app saves stays on
      your device or in your own iCloud.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>Who we are</h2>
    <p>
      Vinyl Corner is made by {company.name}, a company registered in England and Wales (company
      number {company.number}), registered office {company.registeredOffice}. Questions about this
      policy? Email{' '}
      <a href={mailto('Vinyl Corner privacy')} class={link}>
        {contact.email}
      </a>
      .
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>Your music service</h2>
    <p>
      <strong class="font-heavy">Apple Music:</strong> with your permission, the app reads your
      Apple Music library (albums, playlists and play counts), searches the Apple Music catalogue
      and plays music through Apple’s Music app. This goes directly between your device and Apple,
      under{' '}
      <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" class={link}>
        Apple’s privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="font-heavy">Spotify:</strong> if you sign in to Spotify, the app reads your
      saved albums and playlists, searches Spotify’s catalogue and controls playback in the Spotify
      app. Your sign-in is kept in your device’s keychain. This goes directly between your device
      and Spotify, under{' '}
      <a href="https://www.spotify.com/legal/privacy-policy/" class={link}>
        Spotify’s privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </p>
    <p>
      To find new releases and missing album covers, the app also looks up artist and album names in
      Apple’s public iTunes catalogue. These lookups don’t include who you are.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>What stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The app keeps a copy of your library, your shelves, your rankings and your settings on your
      device so it opens quickly. Deleting the app deletes them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="font-heavy">Camera and photos:</strong> when you scan a shelf of records, the
      album names are read from the picture on your device and then searched for in your music
      service. The picture itself isn’t saved or sent anywhere. If you choose a photo instead, the
      app only sees that one photo.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="font-heavy">Apple Intelligence:</strong> where your device supports it, the app
      can tidy album titles using the language model built into your device. This runs on the device
      and nothing is sent off it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong class="font-heavy">Widgets:</strong> what’s playing is shared with the app’s widgets
      through storage on your device.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>iCloud</h2>
    <p>
      Your shelves, rankings, hidden records, chosen covers, what you’ve played and your settings
      sync between your devices through your own private iCloud account. We can’t see this data.
      Each device can stop syncing with “Sync across devices” in the app’s settings, and you can
      delete the app’s iCloud data in your device’s iCloud settings.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>Crash reports</h2>
    <p>
      The app keeps its own crash and performance reports on your device. They’re only sent to us if
      you choose to share them, for example by email when reporting a problem. If you’ve agreed in
      your device’s settings to share analytics with app developers, Apple may also send us
      anonymous crash reports.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you’re testing the app through TestFlight, Apple shares some information with us, such as
      your name and email address if we invited you, your device model and any feedback or crash
      reports you send. See{' '}
      <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/data/en/test-flight/" class={link}>
        Apple’s TestFlight privacy notice
      </a>
      . We only use it to improve the app.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Because we don’t collect information about you, there’s normally nothing for us to give you,
      correct or delete. If you’ve emailed us, you can ask for a copy of that email or for us to
      delete it, and we’ll reply within one month. You also have the right to complain to the
      Information Commissioner’s Office (ICO) at{' '}
      <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" class={link}>
        ico.org.uk
      </a>
      .
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>If we change this policy, we’ll update it here and change the date at the top.</p>
  </PlaceholderPage>
</BaseLayout>
